@model PracticeManagement.Web.Models.PracticeScreen
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "No permission";
}
@*
    Rendered by PracticeController when the signed-in user has no VIEW
    permission for the requested screen.

    It exists because ControllerBase.Forbid() cannot be used here: the
    application registers no authentication scheme (identity lives in the
    session, see Program.cs), so Forbid() throws
    "No authenticationScheme was specified" and the user is shown a stack
    trace instead of an explanation. This view says what is missing and
    who can grant it, and the action still answers HTTP 403.

    Uses the same pm-page-heading / pm-panel primitives as Manage.cshtml
    so it inherits the shell, sidebar and styling with no new CSS.
*@
<div class="pm-page-heading compact">
    <div>
        <p class="pm-eyebrow">@Model.Group / @Model.Title</p>
        <h1>No permission</h1>
    </div>
    <div class="pm-actions">
        <a class="pm-button" asp-controller="Practice" asp-action="Index"><i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-left" aria-hidden="true"></i> Back</a>
    </div>
</div>

<section class="pm-panel">
    <div class="pm-empty">
        <p>
            Your role does not have <strong>View</strong> permission for
            <strong>@Model.Title</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
            An administrator can grant it from
            <strong>Organization &rsaquo; Role Menu Permissions</strong>
            by ticking View for the <code>@Model.Key</code> menu against your role.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>
